INT. CLOCK REPAIR SHOP - DAY

A cramped shop crowded with ticking. Wall clocks, cuckoo clocks, a grandfather in a corner. Brass gears on a velvet tray. A lamp with a magnifying arm. Dust motes drift in the window light.

CLEMENTINE HAWTHORNE-DIAZ (50s, watchmaker's loupe on a headband, apron full of tiny tools) bends over a pocket watch, tweezers in hand. Her assistant BLESSING OYELARAN (20s, sleeves pushed up) sits at a second bench polishing a mainspring. Across the counter stands DETECTIVE MARCELLINE ROUX (40s, trench coat, notebook), who has been silent for some time.

CLEMENTINE
It stopped at 9:47.

ROUX
I know.

CLEMENTINE
Someone smashed the crystal.

ROUX
I know.

CLEMENTINE
And bent the hands. Somebody wanted it to stop there.

ROUX
Yes.

She sets down the tweezers. Blessing looks up. Roux flips a page of her notebook.

ROUX (CONT'D)
When did you sell it?

CLEMENTINE
March. A woman came in. Tall. Hat. She paid in cash.

ROUX
Name?

CLEMENTINE
She didn't give one.

BLESSING
She did.

Clementine turns to her. Blessing shrinks.

BLESSING (CONT'D)
When I wrote the receipt. She said Pettigrew.

ROUX
Pettigrew?

BLESSING
She spelled it. P-E-T-T-I-G-R-E-W.

ROUX
(writing)
That's the victim's name.

A cuckoo clock chimes. The bird shoots out, crying, and retreats. Clementine waits for it to finish before she speaks.

CLEMENTINE
She bought her own watch.

ROUX
Or someone wanted me to think so.

BLESSING
Why would she smash it?

ROUX
Because she wasn't the one who died at 9:47.

Silence. The grandfather clock ticks once. Twice. Roux closes her notebook and gazes at the face of every clock in the room.

Every one of them shows a slightly different time.
